   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.3. Increased capacity of member States to develop gender-responsive legislation, policies and measures for a world of work free from violence and harassment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Building on work carried out in 2019, representatives of the Government, the Federation of Nepal Chamber of Commerce and Industry (FNCCI) and employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organizations formed the C190 Action Group (AG) in February 2021. The objectives of the AG is to develop and coordinate joint actions and measures towards the promotion of C190 and R206 and its effective implementation. Throughout the biennium, the AG, with the support of the ILO, developed and carried out campaigns and initiatives to raise awareness on issues related to violence and harassment at work, and promoting the ratification of C190. 

 

Workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organisations took measures to promote the implementation of C190 among their own members, as well as to build their capacity to prevent and address violence and harassment at work. In particular, the General Federation of Nepalese Trade Unions (GEFONT), Nepal Trade Union Congress (NTUC), and All Nepal Trade Union Federation (ANTUF) and FNCCI embarked on a process to develop a joint model code of conduct to address violence and harassment at work. It will serve as a sample code to prevent and address instances of violence and harassment at the workplace. It will be finalized by Dec 2021 and be promoted for further use by relevant stakeholders. The National Business Initiative (NBI) also developed the code of conduct on violence and harassment, and distributed it to its members to encourage application in their respective organizations. Moreover, GEFONT held several meetings across the countries on the national provisions related, in particular, to sexual harassment and on C190 and R206. It also organised a sensitisation workshop with Parliamentarians and other trade union leaders on the national provisions addressing violence and harassment. NTUC also organised a tripartite meeting on 28 Sept 2021 to sensitive about the importance of ratifying C190 and changes needed in the national legal and policy framework The ILO provided extensive support to constituents throughout the biennium. In particular, through a Japan-funded project, and building on the work carried out in the previous biennium, the ILO provided support to constituents in their efforts to set up the C190 Action Group and build the capacity of its members on issues related to violence and harassment at work and on the principles of C190 and R206. Among others, the project supported the development and rollout of awareness raising campaigns (such as the 16 Days of Activism against Gender-based Violence campaign in 2020) as well as the development of a number of awareness raising or advocacy materials (video messages, social media cards, posters, etc.) as well as C190/R206 translation into local language. The project is currently providing technical support in developing and finalizing both the joint model code of conduct as well as the one led by NBI.  

 

ILO also supported to carry out a study to understand the incidence of violence and harassment in the health sector in Nepal. As policy makers and key stakeholders often do not receive complaints, and do not treat violence and harassment as an issue facing their own workplace, this was part of efforts to make data available to show that violence and harassment is a pervasive issue even though it may be invisible. The findings were shared at the National Dialogue on the Elimination of Violence and Harassment in the World of Work in Nepal on 25 June 2021, organised in collaboration with the C190 Action Group. The event was attended by about 200 participants. The findings will be used to advocate for strengthened action against violence and harassment in the world of work in general, but particularly in the health sector. The C190 Action Group also prepared and presented the Call for Action for the ratification of C190.</narrative>
   </description>
